The '''''Sūn-heng Hâng''''' (順興行) was a Zong family trading house, founded by the paternal grandfather of [[Chiu Bûn-hái]] and owned by his family continuously until 1993. It operated the depot at the bay of [[Oktalwa]] from 1511, built the territory that became the polity of [[Okheng]], and conveyed that territory to the [[Zong colonial administration]] in 1535 for 4,000 taels of gold.

The house was run by Chiu Bûn-hái until his death in 1543 and thereafter by his son [[Chiu Khéng-gio̍k]], who had by then also been the ruler of Okheng, its provisional administrator and — after 1544 — the colony's ambassador to the [[Akwesa]]. It remained in the family's hands for a further three and a half centuries.

Its trade changed with the centuries. The house dealt in rare goods in its early period, moved into precious metals, and entered petrochemicals during the [[Great Adonian War]] of 1935 to 1947. In 1960 it was renamed the Zong Plastics Company. It was acquired by [[Chóng-hòa Chi̍p-thoân]] (總化集團) on 8 June 1993, ending 500 years of family ownership.

== Origins ==

The house was founded by [[Chiu Sūn-heng]], the paternal grandfather of Chiu Bûn-hái, and so before 1474, the year of Chiu Bûn-hái's birth. The date of its establishment is not recorded.

The firm bore the founder's name. Sūn-heng Hâng (順興行) means "Sūn-heng's house", and the family traded under their ancestor's name for some five centuries afterwards. It was a family concern from the outset and remained one throughout. By the beginning of the 16th century it was an established house of [[Huwei]] with the shipping and capital to sustain a settlement at the far end of the [[Shendan Ocean]], and it appears in the colonial papers in 1511 in connection with the grant of land at Oktalwa.

== Oktalwa venture (1511–1535) ==

In 1511 the Akwesa ruler [[Hokta]] granted a plot of land at the bay of Oktalwa to a party of Zong traders in exchange for ten pigs. The grantees were Chiu Bûn-hái and the Sūn-heng Hâng. The transaction was recorded by the Zong as a purchase; Akwesa practice did not treat land as property that could be alienated, and what was granted was the use of the site.

A depot was built on the plot and was operated as a manned outpost from that year. The company assisted the Akwesa in their wars against neighbouring [[Kawesa]] chiefdoms, supplying men, shipping and money, and the territory under Akwesa control expanded in consequence. In 1521 the alliance was sealed by the marriage of Chiu Bûn-hái's son to Hokta's daughter [[Chinowa]].

The territory that resulted was governed jointly by the merchant family and the chiefly family, and became known to the Zong as [[Okheng]] — a name formed from the first element of Oktalwa and the second of Sūn-heng, so that the house's name formed part of the name of the polity it had made.

From the 1520s the house conducted a campaign in Zong to attract settlers to the territory, advertising it to mainland landowners as a place in which land might be bought, held and worked. It sold plots to landowners who remained in Zong, undertook to transport to the territory such labourers from the purchasers' estates as the purchasers chose to send — a body of people of mixed status, the estates of Zong holding both serfs bound to the land and slaves held as property — and acted as managing agent for the holdings it had sold. The proceeds of the campaign — from the sale of plots and from the charges levied on the transport and management of them — funded the development of Okheng: the depot, the works, and the campaigns against neighbouring chiefdoms were paid for out of them.

The campaign also built the house's own expertise. A firm that sold land in one country and moved labour to it from another was in the business of moving people and goods across long distances, which was the business it remained in for the following four centuries. The administration at [[Anping]] judged the arrangement not worth managing when word of it reached there about 1522, and took no action against it for thirteen years.

In 1535 the colonial administration acquired the site as the harbour for its northern presence. Chiu Khéng-gio̍k, by then the ruler of Okheng, was paid 4,000 taels of gold and was at the same time threatened with invasion and with execution for treason should he refuse. No charge was laid. The sum was assessed as the value of the works the company had built, and no allowance was made for the land, the political entity or the trade.

== Family house (1543–1960) ==

Chiu Bûn-hái died at [[Tamsui]] on 9 June 1543, and the house passed to his son. Chiu Khéng-gio̍k was then still the provisional administrator of the territory and had been for eight years; he lost that office in the following year, in the course of the [[Tamsui Plot]], and the company was the position he retained. He held it until his death in 1598, fifty-five years later, during the whole of which period he also served as the colony's ambassador to the Akwesa.

The house remained in the family's ownership throughout, passing between generations of Chiu descendants across the colonial, revolutionary and modern eras of [[Sinchew]]. It was listed on the [[Sinchew Stock Exchange]] on 11 April 1964 under the ticker symbol 'SHH' — the initials of the firm name — and traded under that symbol until its acquisition in 1993, retaining family control throughout. The listing came four years after the renaming of 1960 and twenty-nine years after the house had entered petrochemicals, and it is the point at which a family house of some five centuries became a public company, though one in which the family remained the controlling shareholder.

=== Rare goods ===

The house traded in rare goods in its early period. The trade at Oktalwa on which its 16th-century fortune was built was of this kind: the company exchanged Zong manufactures for the goods of the northwestern coast, and its standing in the colony rested on the unusual commodities it could obtain at the far end of the [[Shendan Ocean]] and supply at [[Anping]].

=== Precious metals ===

At an unrecorded point the house moved from rare goods into precious metals, and it traded in those for the greater part of its existence. This is consistent with the position it had occupied in the colonial economy: a house whose business was the movement of high-value goods across long distances, in a period when the intercontinental trade between Illypnian and Fosian markets ran on silver and gold.

=== Petrochemicals ===

The house moved into petrochemicals during the [[Great Adonian War]] of 1935 to 1947. It became a commodities trading house specialising in the field, a business very different in kind from the trade in which it began, and it was operating in that field when it was renamed.

The move into petroleum products is consistent with the period. The war created an unprecedented demand for petrochemical commodities on all sides, and a house whose business was the long-distance movement of high-value goods was well placed to supply them. A trade in precious metals required the same shipping, credit and market knowledge as a trade in fuels, and the transition from one to the other was one of commodity rather than of kind.

== Zong Plastics Company (1960–1993) ==

In 1960 the house was renamed the Zong Plastics Company (總塑膠公司, Chóng Sok-ka Kong-si). The name reflected the trade it had by then built, and replaced a firm name that had been in continuous use for some five centuries — the founder's own name, dropped after five hundred years of use. It remained a family concern under the new name.

== Acquisition (1993) ==

On 8 June 1993 the company was acquired by [[Chóng-hòa Chi̍p-thoân]] (總化集團), a chemical group, ending 500 years of family ownership. The acquisition followed the logic of the firm's 20th-century business: a petrochemicals trading house acquired by a chemicals manufacturer.

